WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.340
Film Courage: Do you think the first sentence
or scene in a screenplay is important as it

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.340
might be in a novel?

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.140
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher
and Consultant: That’s a good question.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.940
I haven’t thought about it but I would say
no.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.770
Only because I think in a novel…well I don’t
know?

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.230
When you’re reading a novel you’re going
to give it more than one sentence right?

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:25.180
I mean if you’ve picked up a novel to read
you’re probably pretty interested in it

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.630
already.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:33.410
In a screenplay I have heard…so it depends
on the situation.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:39.079
If I am reading this entire script and I already
know I’m reading it front to back then that

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:43.000
first sentence if it’s clunky I’m going
to give it a little leeway.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.580
I’m going to give it another chance right?

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.110
So I’m going to keep reading regardless.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:54.079
I have heard some people say that they can
tell if they are going to keep reading a script

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.079
from the first sentence.

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:58.540
If they read the first sentence and they are
not hooked or it feels like the person doesn’t

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.370
really know what they are doing then they
are going to put it down.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.899
I would say that is an extreme opinion.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:10.789
But you also have to understand that if we’re
talking about a situation where you are submitting

00:01:10.789 --> 00:01:18.679
your script to be read by somebody in the
industry you are asking for their time.

00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:21.539
How much time they are willing to give you
is up to them.

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:25.969
So the best thing you can do is make sure
every sentence of your screenplay is worth

00:01:25.969 --> 00:01:28.369
reading and makes them want to read the next
one right?

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:32.649
You have to set yourself up for success because
there is no guarantee that they are going

00:01:32.649 --> 00:01:34.090
to read your whole script.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.609
Film Courage: That’s fair.

00:01:36.609 --> 00:01:41.039
And this is by the way you said you’re hearing
this from producers or agents or whomever.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:42.759
This is not necessarily your opinion?

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:48.850
Naomi: Right, I mean I very rarely just pick
up a script just to read one sentence and

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.000
see if I like it or not.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:59.039
But it depends on the situation so if someone
is reading a lot of scripts, if they are vetting

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:04.109
scripts for a producer say and they’re just
looking for something really great, they might

00:02:04.109 --> 00:02:08.739
only give it a page or two pages.

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:12.780
Back in the day when I was reading a lot of
scripts for a producer and so I wasn’t reading

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.170
the scripts to try to give notes and try and
make it better.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:21.469
It was just vetting projects to see if there
was anything interesting or worth reading

00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:24.519
the whole script.

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:30.370
It wasn’t uncommon to read the first act
or even to where the inciting incident should

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.900
be and see if anything is happening, see if
there is anything to like sort of catch my

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.140
interest and make me want to keep reading
this script.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:43.470
I think if you’re submitting your scripts
to a producer or a manager or an agent, you

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:49.760
should assume that you need to hook their
attention at least in the first few pages

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.790
if not on the first page itself.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:56.519
Film Courage: And this goes back to the story
you said about being from Oregon and “Hey,

00:02:56.519 --> 00:02:57.519
how’s it going?”

00:02:57.519 --> 00:03:02.120
When you’re working at a production company
and when you called to the Los Angeles area

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.379
[for business purposes in the entertainment
industry] it was more protocol for them to

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:05.379
be like “Yeah?

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:06.930
What do you need?”

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:12.780
Well intentioned I’m sure they are but that’s
the tempo of the industry and it wasn’t

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.599
to find out how your day was going?

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:15.739
Naomi: Yes, exactly.

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:22.109
The pace in Los Angeles, that’s what surprised
me when comparing Oregon to California.

00:03:22.109 --> 00:03:25.810
From Portland to LA the pace was definitely
a lot faster and that took some getting used

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:26.810
to.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.370
But yes I think that speaks to…that’s
exactly right.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.909
People working in the industry are short on
time and there’s a lot of material to get

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:40.580
through and you have to make your material
grab them and make them want to keep reading

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.580
and that is a challenge.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:42.250
Question for the viewers: How much thought
do you put into the first sentence of a screenplay?

